  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1103 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nA rather uninteresting synoptic framework has been presented for\nanalysis through Wednesday morning. The rest of today and \nTuesday will see primarily clear, sunny, and warmer-than-usual \ntemperatures for this time of year. Upper 60's will dominate the\nregion, with portions of SE MO likely to surpass 70. Low south-\nsoutheasterly winds at around 5 knots will contribute to the \nnice weather today. Other than the pleasant temperatures, \ndaytime cloud cover will remain close to 0%, though nighttime \nwill bring some clouds back. Patchy fog will once again be \npossible in the early morning hours Tuesday, quickly eroding \nwith plentiful sunshine.\n\nWednesday appears to be the most eventful day of the week, at\nleast meteorologically speaking. A positively (trending\nneutrally) tilted 250 hPa trough will pass through during the\nevening, and a fairly significant cold front will concurrently\npush through the CWA. Diverse model guidance seems to agree \nwith placement, timing, and orientation of this front. Plenty of\nlift is expected from this front, noted by a sharp Theta-e\ngradient, however, lacking moisture throughout the column will \nseriously inhibit precipitation during this FROPA, especially \nin the western and central portions of the coverage area. That \nbeing said, dew points are trending upwards in subsequent model \nruns since 0z, with a few areas progged at 55-57 degrees. SBCAPE\nis plentiful for a stronger thunderstorm or two, and with 0-3km\nshear at around 30 knots, strong winds associated with linear \nsystems could occur. The main area of interest appears to be \neast of Kentucky Lake for our scope, extending from far S IN, \ndown to S central KY. As time progresses, and moisture return is\nsolidified in the forecast, a more concrete area of convective \ninitiation will be established. Severe risk remains low, but \nplan on the chance of a storm or two.\n\nBehind the cold front, temperatures are expected to fall\nsignificantly, with area-wide highs being 5 to 15 degrees below\naverage through early next week. In comparison to Wednesday's\nhigh temperatures in the low 70s, Thursday will see highs in the\nlow 50s, and Friday even cooler in the high 40s. This is all\nthanks to a strong upper-level low situated solidly over the\nGreat Lakes, whose frigid contents are being accelerated at a \nforecasted 130 knots on the leeward side of a strong ridge,\nleading to strong cold air advection. As this moves out of the \nway, some relief is expected by Sunday, as mid to upper 50s \ntemperatures largely return. No additional precipitation is \nexpected during this Thursday to Sunday period.",
